(:doc :symbol "asl/packages/asl-syntax/tests/asl/layoutTest.asl#" :text "Tests for indented-dialect layout CST and lowering to rd/SExpr")
(:doc :symbol "asl/packages/asl-syntax/tests/asl/layoutTest.asl#testMultilineStringAndCorpusFixtures!" :text "multiline string and corpus fixture parity")
(:doc :symbol "asl/packages/asl-syntax/tests/asl/layoutTest.asl#testNoArrowFnLowersWithoutSentinel" :text "PARSER-inferredUnitConsumers: indentParser.asl's parseFnParamsAndReturn marks a no-arrow fn header with the :inferredUnit sentinel; lowerString/lowerTopFormText must treat that as no return annotation and drop the synthetic -> :inferredUnit pair, not leak the sentinel into the lowered canonical form")
(:doc :symbol "asl/packages/asl-syntax/tests/asl/layoutTest.asl#testArrowedFnBodyNotCorruptedByFalseMatch" :text "INFERUNIT round 2: a prior dropInferredArrowPair matched (rd/sexprHead h) on any SExpr (atom or list) and kept scanning past the real header into the body, so a real-arrow fn whose body happens to contain (-> x) or (:inferredUnit 1) lost those body forms entirely; this fn has a genuine -> Int64 header, so nothing should be dropped at all")
(:doc :symbol "asl/packages/asl-syntax/tests/asl/layoutTest.asl#testParenFnWithNoArrowKeepsListShapedBody" :text "INFERUNIT round 3: testArrowedFnBodyNotCorruptedByFalseMatch never exercises dropInferredArrowPair's own isAtom? guards, because the header's genuine -> atom always stops the scan before it ever reaches the body's list-shaped (-> x). A paren-style fn form, read directly with no indent-dialect header line at all, has no synthetic sentinel inserted and no genuine arrow either: its first two items after params ARE the body's (-> x) and (:inferredUnit 1) lists, so this is the one shape where dropInferredArrowPair must tell a list headed -> from the atom -> (and a list headed :inferredUnit from the atom :inferredUnit); removing either isAtom? guard matches them as the sentinel and drops the whole body")
(:doc :symbol "asl/packages/asl-syntax/tests/asl/layoutTest.asl#testArrowedFnBareSentinelAtomsInBodySurvive" :text "INFERUNIT round 3: testArrowedFnBodyNotCorruptedByFalseMatch never exercises the stop-at-first-mismatch behaviour either, because its body forms are lists, not atoms, so they can never satisfy dropInferredArrowPair's own match check regardless of whether the scan continues past them. A real -> Int64 header followed by two bare atoms -> and :inferredUnit, as plain standalone body forms, is the one shape where continuing to scan past the header's non-matching -> Int64 (instead of stopping there) would reach and wrongly strip them")
